content.text:
Yes. OPF already built its replacement from an earlier ref value, so **native Git CAS provides the protection it needs**: update only if the ref still matches that expected value.

Taking the Entire flock at that point adds waiting without making the prepared result any fresher. In this implementation, that wait can block indefinitely when the context has no deadline.

The relevant code is [persistent_ref_update.go:98](/Users/muskanpaliwal/Work/open-source/entire/cli/fix-opf-ref-cas-race/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go:98):

```go
return retryPersistentRefLockContention(ctx, refName, func() error {
    return gitrepo.CompareAndSwapRef(ctx, repoRoot, refName, newHash, expectedHash)
})
```

That direct call is the bypass—there’s no `withPersistentRefFlock` wrapper.

Normal checkpoint builders still take the Entire flock through [withLockedPersistentRef:76](/Users/muskanpaliwal/Work/open-source/entire/cli/fix-opf-ref-cas-race/cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go:76), because they hold it while building and can rebuild after a CAS conflict.

